1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,918
On the morning of October 2nd, 2001, Zurich Kloten airport woke up to a scene that seemed mathematically impossible.

2
00:00:07,968 --> 00:00:19,966
A single emergency phone call from the fueling depot froze European aviation in its tracks.

3
00:00:20,016 --> 00:00:21,838
BP and Shell maintenance technicians slammed shut the high-pressure fueling valves on Swissair Flight 111.

4
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:30,670
For the first time in 70 years, the Swiss national carrier's corporate credit cards were flatly rejected.

5
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:40,102
Suppliers demanded cold hard cash before pumping a single liter of kerosene. And Swissair had none.

6
00:00:40,152 --> 00:00:50,542
Every single airliner across the international fleet was ordered grounded on the tarmac immediately.

7
00:00:50,592 --> 00:00:58,174
For seven decades, Swissair wasn't just an airline. It was the physical manifestation of Swiss precision.

8
00:00:58,224 --> 00:01:08,206
They called it 'The Flying Bank'—a financial titan backed by banking secrecy and an unblemished triple-A rating.

9
00:01:08,256 --> 00:01:13,030
Its planes were flying embassies of national prestige, serving champagne over the snow-capped Alps.

10
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:20,734
Yet beneath the polished veneer, an aggressive acquisition strategy had metastasized out of control.

11
00:01:20,784 --> 00:01:38,110
Over 17 billion Swiss francs of toxic, hidden off-balance-sheet debt was about to detonate.

12
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:43,270
How did Switzerland's greatest corporate symbol collapse from global titan to bankruptcy in 48 hours?

13
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:52,006
To find the truth, we must peel back the layers of executive hubris, political isolation, and betrayal.

14
00:01:52,056 --> 00:01:59,134
The disaster did not begin with the fuel shutoff in Zurich.

15
00:01:59,184 --> 00:02:08,830
It began nine years earlier, with a fateful decision that cut Switzerland off from Europe.

16
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:17,326
A decision that turned the world's most admired airline into a desperate, hunted animal.

17
00:02:17,376 --> 00:02:23,974
On December 6th, 1992, Swiss citizens cast their ballots in a historic national referendum on joining the European Economic Area.

18
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:25,870
By a razor-thin margin of just 50.3 percent, the Swiss voters said no.

19
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:38,758
In Bern, political traditionalists celebrated the defense of neutrality. But inside Swissair, executives saw a death sentence.

20
00:02:38,808 --> 00:02:47,326
The European Union was deregulating its airspace under the Open Skies directives, allowing member airlines to fly without borders.

21
00:02:47,376 --> 00:02:54,910
British Airways, Lufthansa, and Air France began consolidating, while aggressive budget airlines like Ryanair tore through the market.

22
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:01,342
Swissair was trapped. Because Switzerland was outside the EU, Swissair was legally prohibited from picking up passengers between European cities.

23
00:03:01,392 --> 00:03:07,462
They could not fly Paris to Rome. They could not base aircraft in Frankfurt or Amsterdam.

24
00:03:07,512 --> 00:03:14,422
Every competitor was rapidly expanding their network, while Swissair was confined to a tiny, landlocked domestic island.

25
00:03:14,472 --> 00:03:27,142
Worse, Swiss operating costs were among the highest on earth, paying pilots and staff in strong, expensive Swiss francs.

26
00:03:27,192 --> 00:03:35,830
The board recognized that standing still meant slow, agonizing death by irrelevance.

27
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:51,286
They needed a radical strategy to bypass European protectionist laws.

28
00:03:51,336 --> 00:03:56,566
And in 1997, they found the man who promised to deliver it: Philippe Bruggisser.

29
00:03:56,616 --> 00:04:02,662
Philippe Bruggisser was charismatic, hyper-ambitious, and utterly convinced of his own financial genius.

30
00:04:02,712 --> 00:04:08,398
He presented the board with an audacious expansion blueprint christened 'The Hunter Strategy'.

31
00:04:08,448 --> 00:04:11,494
If European regulators wouldn't let Swissair expand organically, Swissair would buy its way into the European skies.

32
00:04:11,544 --> 00:04:22,702
Because EU law prohibited non-European companies from owning majority stakes, Bruggisser acquired minority 49.5 percent holdings.

33
00:04:22,752 --> 00:04:26,062
Between 1998 and 2000, Swissair went on an uncontrolled multi-billion-franc acquisition spree.

34
00:04:26,112 --> 00:04:29,998
They bought into Belgium's chronically unprofitable national carrier, Sabena, which had lost money for forty consecutive years.

35
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:37,774
They bought stakes in Germany's troubled charter airline LTU, France's Air Littoral and AOM, Portugal's TAP, and Poland's LOT.

36
00:04:37,824 --> 00:04:43,294
Bruggisser assembled a cobbled-together alliance of sick airlines under the banner of the Qualiflyer Group.

37
00:04:43,344 --> 00:04:51,550
To close these deals, Swissair signed confidential, off-balance-sheet shareholder agreements.

38
00:04:51,600 --> 00:05:00,118
These toxic clauses legally bound Swissair to finance the recapitalization and ongoing operating losses of its troubled partners.

39
00:05:00,168 --> 00:05:08,086
Sabena alone was hemorrhaging over one million Swiss francs every single working day.

40
00:05:08,136 --> 00:05:17,158
Swissair had taken on all the financial liabilities of failing foreign carriers, with zero executive authority to fix them.

41
00:05:17,208 --> 00:05:25,150
By January 2001, the bleeding could no longer be disguised behind accounting gymnastics.

42
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,022
Swissair was burning through tens of millions of francs in cash each week just to keep its acquired airlines airborne.

43
00:05:27,072 --> 00:05:31,054
In an emergency midnight board session, Philippe Bruggisser was abruptly fired as Chief Executive.

44
00:05:31,104 --> 00:05:34,798
The board turned to Mario Corti, the widely respected Chief Financial Officer of Nestlé, appointing him Chairman and CEO.

45
00:05:34,798 --> 00:05:54,798
[ARCHIVAL RECORDING // OCT 2001] Mario Corti: Wir sind in einer Situation, in der die Liquiditaet buchstaeblich von Stunde zu Stunde abnimmt... Ohne Ueberbrueckungskredit kann Swissair nicht fliegen.
(We are in a situation where liquidity is evaporating hour by hour... Without a bridge loan, Swissair cannot fly.)

46
00:05:54,798 --> 00:06:14,798
[ARCHIVAL RECORDING // OCT 2001] Mario Corti: Wir sind in einer Situation, in der die Liquiditaet buchstaeblich von Stunde zu Stunde abnimmt... Ohne Ueberbrueckungskredit kann Swissair nicht fliegen.
(We are in a situation where liquidity is evaporating hour by hour... Without a bridge loan, Swissair cannot fly.)

47
00:06:14,798 --> 00:06:34,798
[ARCHIVAL RECORDING // OCT 2001] Mario Corti: Wir sind in einer Situation, in der die Liquiditaet buchstaeblich von Stunde zu Stunde abnimmt... Ohne Ueberbrueckungskredit kann Swissair nicht fliegen.
(We are in a situation where liquidity is evaporating hour by hour... Without a bridge loan, Swissair cannot fly.)

48
00:06:34,798 --> 00:06:54,798
[ARCHIVAL RECORDING // OCT 2001] Mario Corti: Wir sind in einer Situation, in der die Liquiditaet buchstaeblich von Stunde zu Stunde abnimmt... Ohne Ueberbrueckungskredit kann Swissair nicht fliegen.
(We are in a situation where liquidity is evaporating hour by hour... Without a bridge loan, Swissair cannot fly.)

49
00:06:54,848 --> 00:07:04,086
Corti was promised absolute transparency and total autonomy to restructure the battered aviation conglomerate.

50
00:07:04,136 --> 00:07:06,006
He immediately commissioned an exhaustive forensic audit from Ernst & Young to uncover the true financial exposure.

51
00:07:06,056 --> 00:07:17,142
What the auditors discovered shocked the Swiss business establishment to its core.

52
00:07:17,192 --> 00:07:27,846
The true off-balance-sheet commitments and contingent liabilities totaled a catastrophic 17.2 billion Swiss francs.

53
00:07:27,896 --> 00:07:33,942
Then, on September 11th, 2001, commercial aviation suffered the most devastating external shock in modern history.

54
00:07:33,992 --> 00:07:35,814
Transatlantic travel plummeted by over forty percent overnight. Swissair's cash reserves evaporated within days.

55
00:07:35,864 --> 00:07:38,238
Corti knew the parent group was insolvent. He needed an emergency cash bridge of 100 million francs just to survive the week.

56
00:07:38,288 --> 00:07:42,774
He reached out to Switzerland's most powerful banking titans: UBS and Credit Suisse.

57
00:07:42,824 --> 00:07:48,510
Across Zurich's Paradeplatz, behind the closed mahogany doors of UBS, a very different calculus was taking shape.

58
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:55,158
UBS Chairman Marcel Ospel and Credit Suisse leadership had drawn up a confidential rescue blueprint codenamed 'Project Phoenix'.

59
00:07:55,208 --> 00:08:06,318
Project Phoenix was not designed to save Swissair. It was designed to carve out its valuable assets and let the mother company die.

60
00:08:06,368 --> 00:08:16,446
Swissair owned a profitable, debt-free regional subsidiary: Crossair, founded by entrepreneur Moritz Suter.

61
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:18,679
The banks planned to purchase Crossair's shares, transfer Swissair's modern fleet and profitable routes to it, and ditch the 17 billion in debt.

62
00:08:18,729 --> 00:08:25,759
Under the agreement, the banks pledged to provide a liquidity bridge of 250 million francs on Monday, October 1st.

63
00:08:25,809 --> 00:08:37,159
That money was earmarked specifically to keep Swissair planes fueled and operational until the formal handover.

64
00:08:37,209 --> 00:08:44,719
On Monday afternoon, Mario Corti waited for the funds to arrive in Swissair's operational treasury accounts.

65
00:08:44,769 --> 00:08:54,391
The hours ticked by. The funds were deliberately withheld by UBS due to last-minute administrative disputes.

66
00:08:54,441 --> 00:09:02,119
By dawn on Tuesday, October 2nd, the bank transfer had still not arrived.

67
00:09:02,169 --> 00:09:11,215
Fuel suppliers in Zurich, London, and New York issued immediate cash-on-delivery ultimatums.

68
00:09:11,265 --> 00:09:26,623
At 12:30 PM, Swissair Flight Operations received the order: cancel all departures worldwide. The Flying Bank was dead.

69
00:09:26,673 --> 00:09:35,071
The announcement triggered scenes of total pandemonium across international terminals from Tokyo to Los Angeles.

70
00:09:35,121 --> 00:09:43,135
More than 38,000 Swissair passengers and 1,000 flight crew members were stranded on tarmacs without tickets or hotel rooms.

71
00:09:43,185 --> 00:09:51,751
In Zurich, furious Swiss citizens marched on Paradeplatz, hurling eggs at UBS headquarters and denouncing bankers as national traitors.

72
00:09:51,801 --> 00:10:00,943
The Swiss Federal Council was forced to intervene with an emergency taxpayer loan of 450 million francs to resume temporary repatriation flights.

73
00:10:00,993 --> 00:10:09,655
On March 31st, 2002, the final Swissair commercial flight touched down in Zurich, closing 71 years of aviation history.

74
00:10:09,705 --> 00:10:16,447
The profitable remnants of the fleet were rolled into Crossair to form the newly branded Swiss International Air Lines.

75
00:10:16,497 --> 00:10:20,119
Yet the ultimate humiliation was still to come.

76
00:10:20,169 --> 00:10:29,359
Just three years later, in 2005, the newly minted SWISS was acquired by its arch-rival, Germany's Lufthansa, for a fraction of its former value.

77
00:10:29,409 --> 00:10:37,831
In 2007, nineteen former directors and executives, including Bruggisser and Corti, stood trial in Zurich district court.

78
00:10:37,881 --> 00:10:44,191
They faced severe criminal charges of fraudulent bankruptcy, false accounting, and mismanagement.

79
00:10:44,241 --> 00:10:53,167
After months of sensational testimony, the court acquitted every single defendant of criminal wrongdoing, ruling it corporate failure rather than crime.

80
00:10:53,217 --> 00:11:01,807
Seventeen billion francs vanished forever into the Alps—a timeless warning of what happens when executive arrogance meets unbridled financial greed.
